[[File:HMS Tartar (1907) Engine Room.JPG|thumb|right|Engine room of HMS ''Tartar'']]
'''HMS ''Tartar''''' was a {{sclass2|Tribal|destroyer|||1905}} of the [[Royal Navy]] [[Ceremonial ship launching|launched]] in 1907 and sold in 1921. During the [[First World War]], she served in the [[North Sea]] and the [[English Channel]] with the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]].

==Construction and design==
HMS ''Tartar'' was ordered from the [[Southampton]] shipbuilder [[John I. Thornycroft & Company]] under the 1905–06 shipbuilding programme for the Royal Navy, one of five {{sclass2|Tribal|destroyer|||1905}}s ordered under that programme.<ref name="Fried p108">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=108}}</ref> The Tribals derived from a requirement by the [[First Sea Lord]] [[John Fisher, 1st Baron Fisher|"Jackie" Fisher]], for a [[steam turbine]] powered, oil-fueled destroyer capable of at least {{convert|33|kn}}. Armament was specified as three [[QF 12 pounder 12 cwt naval gun|12-pounder ({{convert|3|in|mm|abbr=on|disp=comma}}) 12 cwt guns]]{{#tag:ref|cwt stands for [[hundredweight]], 12 cwt referring to the weight of the gun.|group=lower-alpha}} and two [[British 18 inch torpedo|18-inch (450 mm)]] [[torpedo tube]]s.<ref name="conways06 p71-2">{{Harvnb|Gardiner|Gray|1985|pp=71–72}}</ref><ref name="Fried p107-8">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|pp=107–108}}</ref> While the [[British Admiralty|Admiralty]] laid down the basic requirements, the details of the design of individual ships was left to the builders, although the builder's designs did need to be approved by the [[Director of Naval Construction]] before orders were placed. This meant that individual ships of the class differed significantly from each other.<ref name="conways06 p71-2"/><ref name="Fried p107-8"/>

''Tartar'' was {{convert|274|ft|3|in|m}} long [[length overall|overall]] and {{convert|270|ft|0|in|m}} [[length between perpendiculars|between perpendiculars]], with a [[Beam (nautical)|beam]] of {{convert|26|ft|0|in|m}} and a [[Draft (ship)|draught]] of {{convert|8|ft|4|in|m}}. [[Displacement (ship)|Displacement]] was {{convert|870|LT|t}} normal and {{convert|960|LT|t}} deep load.<ref name="Fried p293">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=293}}</ref> ''Tartar'' had a turtleback [[forecastle]], but with a raised bow compared with {{HMS|Mohawk|1907|2}} (also fitted with a turtleback forecastle), thus avoiding the seakeeping problems that forced ''Mohawk'' to be rebuilt with a raised forecastle.<ref name="Fried p106,9">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|pp=106, 109}}</ref> Six [[Thornycroft boiler]]s (in three boiler rooms) fed steam to [[steam turbine|turbines]] driving three [[propeller]] shafts. The main high-pressure turbine drove the centre shaft, with the outer shafts being fitted with low-pressure turbines, together with cruise and astern turbines. Four [[Funnel (ship)|funnels]] were fitted.<ref name="Fried p108"/><ref name="conways06 p71">{{Harvnb|Gardiner|Gray|1985|p=71}}</ref> The ship's machinery was rated at {{convert|14500|shp|lk=in}}. The ship had a complement of 74 officers and [[Naval rating|ratings]].<ref name="Fried p293"/>

''Tartar'' was [[Keel laying|laid down]] at Thornycroft's  [[Woolston, Southampton|Woolston]] shipyard on 13 November 1905 and [[Ceremonial ship launching|launched]] on 25 June 1907.<ref name="Fried p305">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=305}}</ref> ''Tartar'' made {{convert|36.3|kn|lk=in}} during [[sea trial]]s,<ref name="Fried p108"/> with one run during final trials on 16 December reaching a speed of {{convert|37.037|kn}}, a speed record.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=The Official Trials of the Tartar|magazine=[[The Engineer (UK magazine)|The Engineer]]|date=20 December 1907|page=624|url= http://www.gracesguide.co.uk/images/2/26/Er19071220b.pdf|access-date=28 August 2015}}</ref>{{#tag:ref|Newspaper reports credited ''Tartar'' with speeds as high as {{convert|38|kn}} in 1909.<ref>{{cite news|title=Destroyer's Speed: A World Record|work=The Age|location=[[Melbourne]], Australia|date=18 January 1909|page=7|url=http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article196134735}}</ref>|group=lower-alpha}} She was completed in April 1908.<ref name="Fried p305"/> It had been realised during construction that the armament of the Tribals was too light, so it was decided to strengthen the armament of the first five ships of the class by adding another two 12-pounder guns. The two guns were added to ''Tartar'' following acceptance by the Royal Navy.<ref name="Fried p109-0">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|pp=109–110}}</ref>

==Service==
''Tartar'' formed part of the [[1st Destroyer Flotilla]] of the [[Home Fleet]] when it was formed in 1909 and remained part of this formation until 1913, when she joined the [[4th Destroyer Flotilla]] based at [[HMNB Portsmouth|Portsmouth]].<ref name="nmm">{{cite web|url=http://www.rmg.co.uk/upload/pdf/Warship_Histories_Vessels_iv.pdf|title=NMM, vessel ID 377687|work=Warship Histories, vol iv|publisher=[[National Maritime Museum]]|access-date=28 August 2015|url-status = dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131014122904/http://www.rmg.co.uk/upload/pdf/Warship_Histories_Vessels_iv.pdf|archive-date=14 October 2013|df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref name="manning p25">{{Harvnb|Manning|1961|p=25}}</ref>  In October that year, the Tribals were officially designated the F class, and as such the letter "F" was painted on the bows of the class.<ref name="conways06 p72"/><ref name="Fried p100">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=100}}</ref> In February 1914, the Tribals (including ''Tartar''), as their range was too short for effective open sea operations, were sent to [[Dover]], forming the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]].<ref name="nmm"/><ref name="conways06 p72">{{Harvnb|Gardiner|Gray|1985|p=72}}</ref> On 8 September 1915, the destroyer {{HMS|Leven|1898|2}} was involved in a collision with a transport in the [[English Channel]], suffering a badly damaged [[Bow (ship)|bow]]. She was towed stern first back to [[Dover]], assisted by ''Tartar'', {{HMS|Viking|1909|2}} and the [[Tugboat|tug]] ''Lady Crundall''.<ref name="navhist lost1">{{cite web|title=British Naval Vessels Lost, Damaged and Attacked by Name, 1914-15, some 1916-19|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1NavyBritishShips-Locations10Attacked.htm|work=Ships of the Royal Navy|date=21 June 2016|publisher=Naval-History.net|access-date=2 May 2020}}</ref>

On the night of 26/27 October 1916, German [[torpedo boat]]s of their [[Flanders Flotilla]] carried out a [[Battle of Dover Strait (1916)|large scale raid]] into the English Channel, hoping to attack the drifters watching the anti-submarine nets of the Dover Barrage, and to sink Allied shipping in the Channel. Six Tribal-class destroyers (''Tartar'', ''Mohawk'', ''Viking'', {{HMS|Nubian|1909|2}}, {{HMS|Amazon|1908|2}} and {{HMS|Cossack|1907|2}}) were being held at readiness at Dover as a fast response force,  at readiness in Dover harbour, and when the German 5th Half Flotilla attacked the drifters and sank the old supporting destroyer {{HMS|Flirt|1897|6}}, they were ordered to intervene. The destroyers split up as they left Dover harbour, with ''Viking'' leading ''Mohawk''  and ''Tartar'' from the Western entrance to the port, while the other three destroyers left by the Eastern entrance and failed to join up with ''Viking''{{'}}s group. ''Nubian'' and ''Amazon'' separately ran into the German 17th Half Flotilla, on its way home after sinking the merchant ship ''Queen'', with ''Nubian'' first being badly damaged by a torpedo and ''Amazon'' then heavily hit by German shells. ''Viking''{{'}}s group then encountered a third German formation, the 18th Half Flotilla. ''Mohawk'', second in line of the three British destroyers, was hit by German shells which caused her steering to jam, and turn out of line. ''Tartar'' followed ''Mohawk''{{'}}s turn, while ''Viking''{{'}}s attempts to pursue the German torpedo boats were thwarted when her course was blocked by ''Mohawk''. All the German torpedo boats that took part in the nights operations escaped successfully.<ref name="navopv4 p55-4">{{Harvnb|Newbolt|1928|pp=55–64}}</ref>

''Tartar'' struck a German [[Naval mine|mine]] on 17 June 1917, killing 43 of her crew, including her newly appointed [[Captain (naval)|captain]], G. K. Twiss, but was towed to safety.<ref name="nmm"/><ref name="baconv2 p361,625">{{Harvnb|Bacon|1919|pp=361, 625}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Kindell|first=Don|title=1st - 30th June 1917 in date, ship/unit & name order|work=World War 1 - Casualty Lists of the Royal Navy and Dominion Navies|publisher=Naval-History.net|url=http://www.naval-history.net/xDKCas1917-06Jun.htm|access-date=28 August 2015}}</ref> By November 1917, ''Tartar'' had joined the [[11th Destroyer Flotilla]], based at [[Blyth, Northumberland]].<ref>{{cite journal|title=Supplement to the Navy List Showing Organisation of the Fleet, Flag Officers' Commands &c.: I.—The Grand Fleet: Tenth Submarine Flotilla|journal=The Navy List|date=November 1917|page=12|url=http://digital.nls.uk/british-military-lists/pageturner.cfm?id=92177074}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Ships of the Royal Navy - Location/Action Date, 1914–1918: Part 2 - Admiralty "Pink Lists", 2 January 1918|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1NavyBritishShips-Locations2PL1801.htm|publisher=Naval-History.net|access-date=28 August 2015}}</ref> On 18 February 1918, ''Tartar'' collided with the merchant ship '' Ardgantock'' off [[West Hartlepool]], sinking the merchant ship.<ref name="navhist lost1"/> ''Tartar'' still served with the 11th Submarine Flotilla on 11 November 1918, when the [[Armistice of 11 November 1918|Armistice]] ended fighting between the Allies and Germany.<ref>{{cite web|title=Ships of the Royal Navy - Location/Action Date, 1914–1918: Part 2 - Admiralty "Pink Lists", 11 November 1918|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1NavyBritishShips-Locations2PL1811.htm|publisher=Naval-History.net|access-date=14 August 2015}}</ref>

==Disposal==
''Tartar'' was sold for [[Ship breaking|scrap]] on 9 May 1919 to [[Thos. W. Ward]] of [[Hayle]].<ref name="ditt p60">{{Harvnb|Dittmar|Colledge|1972|p=60}}</ref>

